y value above 458 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': "**Decision Time: Texas Hold'em Turn**\n\nAlright, I'm in hand three of this session, and things aren't looking great. Down a bit, and my current holding is 5 of spades and Queen of clubs. The board shows 3 spades, 10 diamonds, 4 hearts, and now an Ace of clubs on the turn. I've got a small stack left, around 19,900 chips.  It looks like I'm out of position, playing the Big Blind, which means my action is after the opponent's.\n\nMy opponent, after checking pre-flop and on the flop, just bet 229 into a small pot. I'm sitting on Ace high, but with a Queen kicker. But really, the board has an Ace and everyone essentially has an Ace high.\n\nI've got a gutshot straight draw, needing a 2 to hit the Wheel. That's only 4 outs, about an 8.7% chance to hit. Even if I hit, will I get paid? Their line is suspicious - check, check, then a pot-sized bet after the Ace hits. They could be representing strength, or they could be bluffing on the scare card.\n\nI've got a low chance of improving, and my current showdown value is weak.  I have Ace high, but it's vulnerable to any higher card, a pair, or two pair. If they have a king or better, I lose. It is risky to call with such a small chance.\n\nGiven the small pot, and their betting the Ace, it's likely they have an Ace or they are trying to steal. Calling would be a -EV play.  I'm folding.  It's just not worth the risk here.\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation:\nI currently hold the Queen of Clubs and 5 of Spades. The board is 3s, 10d, 4h, Ac.\nThis gives me a pair of Aces (using the board's Ace) with a Queen kicker. I also have a gutshot straight draw (A-3-4-5 on the board/hand, needing a 2 to complete the A-2-3-4-5 straight).\nThe opponent checked the flop but bet 229 on the turn when the Ace appeared. This indicates they likely have an Ace (giving them a better kicker or the same hand) or hit two pair (e.g., A3, A4, A10). Alternatively, they are bluffing at the scare card.\nHowever, my equity is low. I only have 4 outs (the four 2s) to hit the straight, roughly an 8-9% chance on the river. My current hand strength (Pair of Aces, Queen kicker) is decent but often dominated if the opponent actually has an Ace (often accompanied by a King or better kicker) or two pair. Calling a pot-sized bet with only a gutshot and a marginal made hand is statistically a losing play in the long run.\n\nReason:\nThe pot odds do not justify chasing a 4-out straight draw (approx. 9% chance). The opponent's bet on the Ace signals strength that likely beats my Queen kicker, and I do not have enough equity to continue. Preserving chips for a better spot is the optimal strategy.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
